and $C_i:=X_i^*\cap \Gamma(y_i^*)$.
Since $(X_i^*, Y_i^*)$ is $(4\ve, d/2)$-super-regular,
we have $|B_i|, |C_i|\ge d|X_i^*|/2>d|X_i^*|/4$.
Recall from Claim~\ref{hamiltonian_path} that
$\{X_1, Y_1\}=\{V_{x_1}, V_{x_2}\}$ and $\{X_k, Y_k\}=\{V_{y_1}, V_{y_2}\}$. We assume, w.l.o.g., that $X_1=
V_{x_1}$ and $X_k=V_{y_1}$.
Let $A_{1}=X_1^*\cap \Gamma(x_1)$, $B_{1}=Y_1^*\cap \Gamma(x_2)$, $C_{k}=X_k^*\cap \Gamma(y_1)$, and $D_{k}=Y_k^*\cap \Gamma(y_2)$.  Since $deg(x_1, X_1)\ge (d-\ve)N$, we have
$deg(x_1, X^*_1)\ge (d-\ve-2\ve-2d^2)N\ge d|X_1^*|/4$,
and thus $|A_1|\ge d|X_1^*|/4$.
Similarly, we have $|B_1|, |C_k|, |D_k|\ge d|X_1^*|/4$.
For each $1\le i\le k$, we assume that $L_{x}^i=a_1^ib_1^i-L_{x}^i-c_1^id_1^i$ and
$L_{y}^i=a_2^ib_2^i-L_{y}^i-c_2^id_2^i$,
