-NOTES = r"""
-Definition and paper objects. Section 1 defines a Halin graph H=T union C,
-where T is a spanning tree with no degree-two vertex and C is a cycle on
-exactly the leaves in the planar order. Section 2 defines the ladder L_n and
-the ladder-based Halin graphs H_1 through H_5. Proposition 1 joins two H_1 or
-H_2 graphs after deleting their leaf edges x_i y_i and adding the three edges
-x_1x_2, y_1y_2, and u_1u_2; its proof explicitly gives the new underlying tree
-and leaf cycle. Section 3 defines Extremal Case 1 by an approximate cut into
-two dense pieces. Theorem 2 (labelled ``extremal1'' in the source) constructs
-exactly a spanning graph Q from Proposition 1 in that regime.
+NOTES = """\
+Section 1 fixes the exact definition: a Halin graph is a simple planar graph
+T union C, where T is a tree on at least four vertices with no degree-2
+vertex and C is a cycle on precisely T's leaves.  The same section says that
+unrestricted (spanning) Halin containment is NP-complete, but that worst-case
+fact does not establish Track-A hardness for a planted distribution.  Theorem
+1.1 is an asymptotic existence result at minimum degree (n+1)/2 and its proof
+uses Regularity and Blow-up lemmas; this sparse family therefore does not claim
+to instantiate that theorem.
 
-Step-0 decision. The paper says that spanning-Halin recognition is NP-complete
-in general, but worst-case hardness does not apply to the generated promise
-distribution. The paper's main Dirac theorem is also existential and its
-non-extremal proof invokes the Regularity and Blow-up Lemmas. Therefore neither
-fact licenses a Track-A claim. This module is explicitly Track B. Its successful
-reference algorithm scans all O(n^2) pairs to build and two-colour the complement.
-The compact route examines the nonneighbors of one vertex and one opposite
-vertex; only four exceptional cross-neighbors remain, and three same-side
-anchors classify each of them. At n=120 this is at most 2n+12=252 exact
-adjacency decisions after the complement insight.
+Instead, Definition 1 and the H1 construction in Section 2 provide the native
