    20	
    21	## Why Track B
    22	
    23	The paper says in Section 1 that unrestricted spanning-Halin containment is NP-complete, but that worst-case statement does not justify Track A for this promise distribution. The main Dirac theorem (Theorem 1.1), and the Extremal Case 1 result in Section 3, are existence results; the non-extremal proof also invokes the Regularity and Blow-up Lemmas. This module therefore names the efficient recovery method rather than hiding it.
    24	
    25	The successful reference algorithm enumerates the bounded `Q` templates, applies exact bounded-degree graph isomorphism, and interpolates an affine map. It is `O(N^4)` for this declared language. At the shipping preset it solved 8/8, took at most **1,252,040 counted operations**, and averaged **4.971 s** per instance. Once the topology and XOR invariant are recognized, a one-pass topological labelling plus seven-bit affine-basis interpolation takes **187 operations** and averaged **0.220 s**, also solving 8/8. The benchmark is this mechanical-to-compact gap, not a complexity-theoretic hardness claim.
    26	
    27	## Worked demo
    28	
    29	This is the complete rendered graph data for `make_instance(n=16, seed=0)` (the surrounding statement defines the `Q` template and affine formula exactly):
